lisa-design:
- GOPRIVATE is set in the env of every command ccf-bump's runner execs (go get, go mod tidy and the ui sync script), not only "go": a sync script may run go too, and the pattern is harmless otherwise. Pattern is github.com/<--owner>/*, so --owner keeps working.
- Existing GOPRIVATE is appended to (deduped), never clobbered. GONOPROXY/GONOSUMDB are appended to only when set non-empty, because when set they override GOPRIVATE; unset they follow it. Only the env var is read, not a `go env -w` file (CI has none).
- No credentials are added for the direct fetch: the org repos are public and run1 already strips GH_TOKEN/GITHUB_TOKEN; workflows use persist-credentials: false. Verified locally that an anonymous direct fetch (GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null, no tokens) of gooci@latest works.
- Dry-run skip lives in ccf-bump (bumper.tagged, --dry-run only), not in the train: ccf-bump knows which repos actually pin a Go module, so only those are affected; it returns an error after printing the plan, which the train already reports as "ccf-bump couldn't plan (planned versions aren't tagged yet?)" and treats as bumped, i.e. the existing dry-run semantics are kept.
- The tag check is limited to go.mod changes (Change.GoModule): only those run go get; text-only updaters never ask the proxy. Live runs are unchanged (the train bumps after the release, so the tag exists).
- Tests use a fake `go` on PATH that logs args and env, so they assert the real exec env and that go never ran, without a test-only runner hook in production code.
